WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.532 --> 00:00:20.942
Hello, and welcome to the episode of Superstars in History, the talk show that features interviews with famous

2
00:00:21.022 --> 00:00:22.723
people from the past.

3
00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:28.606
Today's guest is a first for our show—she's a real saint!

4
00:00:29.447 --> 00:00:36.855
A Catholic nun and missionary, this hardworking woman devoted her life to helping the world's poorest people.

5
00:00:37.856 --> 00:00:44.104
Straight from history, here is one of my favorite superstars Mother Teresa!

6
00:01:01.432 --> 00:01:02.273
Hello, Faye.

7
00:01:03.174 --> 00:01:07.738
Mother Teresa, it's so great to have you here on Superstars in History!

8
00:01:07.758 --> 00:01:09.920
Oh no, Faye!

9
00:01:10.001 --> 00:01:11.442
I'm not a superstar.

10
00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:17.748
During my lifetime I just tried to follow a simple path—reaching out to the poor.

11
00:01:18.809 --> 00:01:25.616
You're very humble, Mother Teresa, but we can't wait to learn more about your extraordinary life.

12
00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:32.030
You were born in 1910 in a town in what is now North Macedonia.

13
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:33.770
That's right.

14
00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:41.552
My Albanian parents named me Agnes Gonxha Bojaxhiu, and I grew up in a house with a lovely

15
00:01:41.612 --> 00:01:43.133
garden and fruit trees.

16
00:01:44.273 --> 00:01:48.854
My parents were devout Catholics who were always very generous to the poor.

17
00:01:49.914 --> 00:01:51.915
I was especially close to my mother.

18
00:01:52.815 --> 00:01:58.177
She taught my brother, sister, and me to love our neighbors and share what we had.

19
00:01:59.198 --> 00:02:02.839
We were a happy family until tragedy struck.

20
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:04.680
Oh no.

21
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:06.080
What happened?

22
00:02:07.081 --> 00:02:12.643
When I was about eight, my father died unexpectedly, which left us grief-stricken.

23
00:02:13.523 --> 00:02:19.426
My mother was forced to earn money for our family so she started her own business selling embroidered

24
00:02:19.486 --> 00:02:28.025
cloth. It was a hard time, yet Mother still remembered others, often visiting the poor and quietly caring

25
00:02:28.105 --> 00:02:31.667
for an elderly neighbor who was very sick with alcoholism.

26
00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:34.548
We also went to church frequently.

27
00:02:35.448 --> 00:02:40.270
I joined a youth group, where I heard beautiful stories about missionaries.

28
00:02:41.331 --> 00:02:45.793
I especially loved hearing about their work with children in India.

29
00:02:46.793 --> 00:02:50.094
By age 12 I felt a vocation toward the poor.

30
00:02:51.015 --> 00:02:53.456
I decided to become a missionary myself.

31
00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.542
Six years later you left home to join an order of nuns.

32
00:02:59.463 --> 00:03:05.366
Yes, I joined the Sisters of Our Lady of Loreto, known for their missionary work in India.

33
00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:13.390
But before traveling to India, I joined the sisters in Ireland to learn English, the language spoken by

34
00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:13.950
the order.

35
00:03:15.691 --> 00:03:22.635
In 1928, when I said good-bye to my mother and departed for Ireland, it was the last time

36
00:03:22.795 --> 00:03:23.956
I ever saw her.

37
00:03:25.336 --> 00:03:26.737
That's so sad!

38
00:03:27.737 --> 00:03:29.098
It was sad indeed.

39
00:03:30.338 --> 00:03:34.620
Yet doing God's work often requires families to make sacrifices.

40
00:03:35.921 --> 00:03:39.382
After about two months in Ireland, I set sail for India.

41
00:03:40.502 --> 00:03:46.545
As a novice, or nun in training, I worked in Darjeeling, where I taught poor children each day

42
00:03:46.925 --> 00:03:50.667
and learned two Indian languages, Hindi and Bengali.

43
00:03:51.827 --> 00:03:53.868
When did you officially become a nun?

44
00:03:54.812 --> 00:03:56.933
I took my vows in 1931.

45
00:03:58.074 --> 00:04:05.799
I chose to be called Sister Teresa because I had always admired Saint Therese, a French nun who'd

46
00:04:05.899 --> 00:04:08.300
devoted herself to praying for missionaries.

47
00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:15.905
I decided to spell it the Spanish way—"Teresa"—because another nun had already taken "Therese."

48
00:04:16.865 --> 00:04:21.228
Soon afterward I was sent to the city of Calcutta to teach at a high school.

49
00:04:22.396 --> 00:04:27.738
I worked there for 17 happy years, teaching and later becoming principal.

50
00:04:28.818 --> 00:04:30.998
Then one day the call arrived.

51
00:04:32.279 --> 00:04:34.319
A phone call, Mother Teresa?

52
00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:37.680
Oh no, Faye.

53
00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.461
It was an inner call.

54
00:04:40.621 --> 00:04:44.982
I was traveling on a train when I suddenly heard a very clear inner voice.

55
00:04:46.123 --> 00:04:52.905
It was God commanding me to renounce my life by leaving the convent to work with the poor,

56
00:04:53.285 --> 00:04:55.246
living among them in the slums.

57
00:04:56.346 --> 00:05:02.568
I got permission from the church to leave the convent, I traded my nun's habit for a simple

58
00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:08.451
white-and-blue sari, the traditional Indian dress, and I moved to the slums.

59
00:05:09.991 --> 00:05:11.492
That was in 1948.

60
00:05:12.292 --> 00:05:14.453
What was Calcutta like back then?

61
00:05:15.534 --> 00:05:21.136
Because of political events, there had been a huge influx of refugees from Pakistan.

62
00:05:22.132 --> 00:05:26.794
Calcutta was packed with shanty towns made of cardboard and mud.

63
00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:33.717
Sewage poured into open drains in the street, and garbage was left there, rotting.

64
00:05:34.677 --> 00:05:37.498
Many people lived in the streets, begging for food.

65
00:05:38.459 --> 00:05:42.560
Deadly diseases such as tuberculosis and leprosy were rampant.

66
00:05:43.581 --> 00:05:45.241
That sounds awful.

67
00:05:45.742 --> 00:05:48.083
How did you start helping people on the streets?

68
00:05:49.163 --> 00:05:51.004
At first I didn't know what to do!

69
00:05:52.072 --> 00:05:55.633
So I started doing what I knew best teaching children.

70
00:05:56.673 --> 00:06:00.094
But this time my classroom was in the streets of Calcutta.

71
00:06:01.115 --> 00:06:09.297
Since I didn't have anything—not chalk nor books nor paper—I just picked up a stick and scribbled letters

72
00:06:09.377 --> 00:06:09.917
in the dirt.

73
00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:15.919
My little school grew quickly, and soon I was given funds to rent a building.

74
00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:20.160
My next step was to open a clinic to treat the sick.

75
00:06:21.368 --> 00:06:24.509
Over time other nuns joined me in my mission.

76
00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:31.352
We looked for people who were dying in the streets and carried them to our home, where they

77
00:06:31.412 --> 00:06:33.813
could die with peace and dignity.

78
00:06:35.054 --> 00:06:42.777
In 1950 the Vatican recognized you and the other missionaries as an order—the Missionaries of Charity.

79
00:06:43.777 --> 00:06:50.340
Yes, our mission spread to other countries, and we ran clinics, orphanages, and soup kitchens.

80
00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:58.842
In addition to other vows, we took a vow of poverty, because to know the poor, Faye, you

81
00:06:58.962 --> 00:07:00.423
must be poor yourself.

82
00:07:01.523 --> 00:07:07.145
We lived in the slums, sleeping on thin mattresses and owning few possessions.

83
00:07:08.245 --> 00:07:12.107
Each sister had only three saris, which we washed by hand.

84
00:07:13.147 --> 00:07:20.355
You always treated the poor and sick with dignity, kissing the hands of people with leprosy and hugging

85
00:07:20.515 --> 00:07:22.417
patients who were dying of AIDS.

86
00:07:23.178 --> 00:07:29.904
When you walked through the streets of Calcutta, people bent to touch your feet—a sign of deep respect.

87
00:07:30.764 --> 00:07:34.988
Then in 1979, you won the Nobel Peace Prize.

88
00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:37.030
What an honor!

89
00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:40.693
I was not worthy of that honor, Faye.

90
00:07:41.674 --> 00:07:46.902
As I often told others, I was nothing, only a pencil in God's hands.

91
00:07:48.163 --> 00:07:50.685
I believe I've said enough about myself for now.

92
00:07:51.726 --> 00:07:52.487
Thank you, Faye.

93
00:07:53.207 --> 00:07:55.629
I do appreciate your interest in our mission.

94
00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.991
Good-bye, Mother Teresa.

95
00:07:58.351 --> 00:08:00.233
It was a privilege to meet you!

96
00:08:01.214 --> 00:08:03.215
We'll let you return to history now.

97
00:08:04.176 --> 00:08:13.224
Good-bye. By the 1990s, doctors were urging Mother Teresa to retire.

98
00:08:14.092 --> 00:08:20.838
But she refused, continuing to work and serve the poor, even with serious health problems such as heart

99
00:08:20.878 --> 00:08:26.762
disease. In 1997 she died of a heart attack at age 87.

100
00:08:27.983 --> 00:08:36.931
Often called a living saint, Mother Teresa was named an official saint—Saint Teresa of Calcutta—by the Catholic Church

101
00:08:37.031 --> 00:08:37.271
in 2016.

102
00:08:39.124 --> 00:08:44.066
Today her order, the Missionaries of Charity, still carries on her mission.

103
00:08:45.006 --> 00:08:49.287
I hope my interview with Mother Teresa was interesting and inspiring!

104
00:08:50.168 --> 00:08:53.309
I'll be back soon with another guest from the
